<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SECTION 2 — DEV MODE (ALL PLATFORMS)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s2">
<h2><span class="snum">02</span> Dev Mode — Direct Python Launch <span class="ptag p-all">All platforms</span></h2>

<p>This is what you use every day as the developer. The project uses <strong>uv</strong> to manage the virtual environment, but plain pip works too.</p>

<ol class="steps">
  <li>
    <strong>Install Python 3.9+</strong> — <span class="ptag p-win">Win</span> use the Microsoft Store or <code>winget install Python.Python.3.11</code>. <span class="ptag p-mac">Mac</span> <code>brew install python@3.11</code> or the official .pkg. <span class="ptag p-lnx">Linux</span> <code>sudo apt install python3.11</code> or <code>dnf install python3</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Install uv</strong> (recommended):
    <pre><span class="cm"># macOS / Linux</span>
<span class="cmd">curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh</span>

<span class="cm"># Windows (PowerShell)</span>
<span class="cmd">powershell -c "irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex"</span></pre>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Clone &amp; enter the project</strong>:
    <pre><span class="cmd">git clone &lt;repo-url&gt; Silmulator</span>
<span class="cmd">cd Silmulator/Silmulator</span></pre>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Install dependencies</strong>:
    <pre><span class="cm"># with uv (creates .venv automatically)</span>
<span class="cmd">uv sync</span>

<span class="cm"># OR with pip</span>
<span class="cmd">pip install -r requirements.txt</span></pre>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Launch</strong>:
    <pre><span class="cm"># with uv</span>
<span class="cmd">uv run python main.py</span>

<span class="cm"># OR with activated venv</span>
<span class="cmd">source .venv/bin/activate</span>   <span class="cm"># macOS / Linux</span>
<span class="cmd">.venv\Scripts\activate</span>       <span class="cm"># Windows</span>
<span class="cmd">python main.py</span></pre>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>Where does this lead in code? <code>main.py</code> is a two-liner:</p>
<pre><span class="cm"># Silmulator/main.py</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> sys
<span class="kw">from</span> silmulator.app <span class="kw">import</span> main   <span class="cm"># → silmulator/app.py:main()</span>

<span class="kw">if</span> __name__ == <span class="str">"__main__"</span>:
    sys.exit(main())               <span class="cm"># exits with the Qt event loop return code</span></pre>

<div class="callout tip">
  <span class="callout-icon">💡</span>
  <div>After <code>pip install -e .</code> (editable install), the command <code>silmulator</code> becomes available as a terminal command on any platform — it calls the same <code>silmulator.app:main</code> via <code>pyproject.toml</code>'s <code>[project.scripts]</code> entry.</div>
</div>

<button class="mark-done" onclick="markDone('s2',this)">○ Mark complete</button>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SECTION 3 — DOCKER (ANY OS, BROWSER)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s3">
<h2><span class="snum">03</span> Docker — Browser Launch <span class="ptag p-docker">Any OS</span></h2>

<p>Docker is the easiest cross-platform method: no Python install, no display drivers, no PyQt6 version headaches. The container runs Xvfb (virtual framebuffer) + VNC + NoVNC, and you connect via a browser.</p>

<ol class="steps">
  <li>
    <strong>Install Docker Desktop</strong> for your platform. On Linux, Docker Engine is enough.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Start the container</strong>:
    <pre><span class="cmd">cd Silmulator/</span>   <span class="cm"># repo root, where docker-compose.yml lives</span>
<span class="cmd">docker compose up -d</span></pre>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Open in browser</strong>: navigate to <code>http://localhost:6080/vnc.html</code>. Password: <code>silmulator</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Stop</strong>: <code>docker compose down</code>. Saved layouts persist in the <code>silmulator-config</code> named volume.
  </li>
</ol>

<p>Useful variants:</p>
<pre><span class="cm"># Change port if 6080 is taken</span>
<span class="env">APP_PORT</span>=8080 <span class="cmd">docker compose up -d</span>

<span class="cm"># Change the VNC password</span>
<span class="env">VNC_PASSWORD</span>=mysecret <span class="cmd">docker compose up -d</span>

<span class="cm"># Check health</span>
<span class="cmd">docker compose ps</span>        <span class="cm"># shows "healthy" once VNC is ready</span>
<span class="cmd">docker compose logs -f</span>   <span class="cm"># stream logs; look for "x11vnc is ready"</span></pre>

<div class="callout info">
  <span class="callout-icon">ℹ️</span>
  <div>The production Dockerfile is a <strong>multi-stage build</strong>: stage 1 compiles the C++ SCPI extension (<code>scpi_core.so</code>) inside a builder image; stage 2 copies only the <code>.so</code> file into a slim runtime image with Qt6 and NoVNC. This keeps the final image small while bundling the optional C++ speedup automatically.</div>
</div>

<button class="mark-done" onclick="markDone('s3',this)">○ Mark complete</button>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     SECTION 4 — STANDALONE INSTALLERS
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s4">
<h2><span class="snum">04</span> Standalone Installers — Ship to End Users</h2>

<p>All three installer builds use <strong>PyInstaller</strong> (<code>silmulator.spec</code>) to bundle the Python interpreter, all dependencies, and the app into a single artifact that users can run without installing Python.</p>

<h3 style="font-size:.95rem;color:#fff;margin:1.2rem 0 .6rem"><span class="ptag p-mac">macOS</span> → .dmg</h3>

<ol class="steps">
  <li>Ensure Python 3.9+ and optionally <code>brew install create-dmg</code> are available.</li>
  <li>Run: <pre><span class="cmd">./build_installer.sh</span></pre></li>
  <li>Output: <code>dist/Silmulator-1.0.0-macos-arm64.dmg</code></li>
  <li>Distribute the DMG; users drag <code>Silmulator.app</code> to their Applications folder.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout warn-box">
  <span class="callout-icon">⚠️</span>
  <div>macOS Gatekeeper will block unsigned apps on first launch. The user must right-click → Open to bypass this, or you need an Apple Developer certificate + <code>codesign</code> + <code>notarytool</code> for a smooth out-of-box experience.</div>
</div>

<h3 style="font-size:.95rem;color:#fff;margin:1.2rem 0 .6rem"><span class="ptag p-lnx">Linux</span> → .AppImage</h3>

<ol class="steps">
  <li>Run: <pre><span class="cmd">./build_installer.sh</span></pre>
    (The script downloads <code>appimagetool</code> automatically if it's missing.)</li>
  <li>Output: <code>dist/Silmulator-1.0.0-linux-x86_64.AppImage</code></li>
  <li>Distribute the AppImage; users mark it executable and run it:
    <pre><span class="cmd">chmod +x Silmulator-1.0.0-linux-x86_64.AppImage</span>
<span class="cmd">./Silmulator-1.0.0-linux-x86_64.AppImage</span></pre>
  </li>
</ol>

<h3 style="font-size:.95rem;color:#fff;margin:1.2rem 0 .6rem"><span class="ptag p-win">Windows</span> → .exe or .zip</h3>

<ol class="steps">
  <li>Open Command Prompt (not PowerShell — the script is a <code>.bat</code>).</li>
  <li>Optionally install <a href="https://nsis.sourceforge.io/Download" style="color:var(--accent)">NSIS 3.x</a> for a proper installer <code>.exe</code>.</li>
  <li>Run: <pre><span class="cmd">build_installer.bat</span></pre></li>
  <li>Output (with NSIS): <code>dist\Silmulator-1.0.0-windows-installer.exe</code><br>
      Output (without NSIS): <code>dist\Silmulator-1.0.0-windows.zip</code> — unzip and run <code>Silmulator.exe</code> inside.</li>
</ol>

