<div class="quiz-section" id="s6">
<h2>Check Your Understanding</h2>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q1. You want to run Silmulator on a remote Linux CI server with no display. Which launch method works?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="python main.py requires a real display server; it will fail with 'cannot connect to X server' in a headless environment.">python main.py directly on the server</li>
    <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. Docker with NoVNC runs a virtual framebuffer (Xvfb) inside the container. The browser is the display — no physical screen or X server needed on the host.">docker compose up -d, then access via browser at :6080</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="AppImage needs a native display to open a Qt window. It will fail the same way as python main.py on a headless server.">Run the Linux AppImage on the server</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="uv run python main.py still launches the Qt GUI, which requires a display. uv only manages the Python environment, not the display.">uv run python main.py — uv handles the display setup</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback"></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q2. After running <code>pip install -e .</code>, which command launches Silmulator?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="silmulator-gui is also registered in pyproject.toml as a gui-script, but 'silmulator' alone is the canonical command defined under [project.scripts].">silmulator-gui (the gui-script entry is the only one)</li>
    <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. pyproject.toml registers 'silmulator' under [project.scripts] pointing to silmulator.app:main — pip install creates this shim on PATH.">silmulator</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="python main.py works but it's the pre-install path. After pip install -e . the 'silmulator' command on PATH is the right tool.">python main.py (the installed entry-point is only for packaged releases)</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="There is no 'sil' command registered. The script name comes from the [project.scripts] key in pyproject.toml.">sil (short alias registered in setup.py)</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback"></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q3. On macOS, a user double-clicks <code>Silmulator.app</code> from the DMG and gets "Silmulator can't be opened because Apple cannot check it for malicious software." What is the quickest fix that does NOT require code-signing?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Disabling Gatekeeper entirely (spctl --master-disable) is heavy-handed and removes all app security checks system-wide. Not appropriate for one app.">Disable Gatekeeper entirely: sudo spctl --master-disable</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The app already has execute permission from the DMG build; chmod +x is for AppImages and scripts, not .app bundles on macOS.">Run chmod +x Silmulator.app in Terminal first</li>
    <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. Right-click (or Control-click) → Open bypasses Gatekeeper's quarantine for unsigned apps. The user is asked to explicitly confirm once; subsequent launches open normally.">Right-click (Control-click) → Open → confirm in the dialog</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Moving the app outside the DMG to Applications first doesn't remove the quarantine xattr — the same Gatekeeper block still fires.">Drag to Applications first, then double-click from there</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback"></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q4. The Windows installer build produces a <code>.zip</code> instead of a <code>.exe</code> installer. Why?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="PyInstaller produces a folder of files by default. The .bat script packages those into a zip or .exe; it is not PyInstaller that chooses between the two.">PyInstaller on Windows always outputs a .zip; NSIS converts it to .exe later in a separate step.</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Python version has no bearing on the installer format. The .zip vs .exe choice is determined by whether NSIS (makensis) is found on PATH.">Python is below 3.11, so NSIS cannot produce an .exe for that version.</li>
    <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. build_installer.bat checks whether makensis.exe is on PATH (or in the default NSIS install location). If it's missing, the script falls back to zipping the PyInstaller output folder.">NSIS (makensis) is not installed or not on PATH — the .bat falls back to zipping the PyInstaller output.</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The .bat file has no such check. It attempts the .exe build if NSIS is present and falls back to .zip if not, regardless of which Windows version is running.">The machine is running Windows 10 or below; the .exe installer requires Windows 11.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback"></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q5. You clone the repo on macOS and run <code>uv sync</code> — what does that do, specifically?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="uv sync creates (or updates) the .venv from uv.lock / pyproject.toml. It does not run any build steps or compile the C++ extension.">Compiles the C++ SCPI extension and installs all Python deps into the system Python.</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="uv sync reads uv.lock and installs exactly those versions. 'uv add' or 'uv lock --upgrade' is what updates the lockfile.">Updates uv.lock to the latest package versions and installs them.</li>
    <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. uv sync reads uv.lock (or pyproject.toml if no lockfile) and installs the pinned set of packages into the project's .venv, creating it if it doesn't exist yet.">Creates (or updates) .venv with the exact package versions from uv.lock, without touching the system Python.</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="uv sync is a local project command; it does not push or pull from any remote. 'uv publish' is for uploading packages.">Syncs the local package cache with the PyPI remote index — downloads any packages that are out of date.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback"></p>
</div>

<div class="quiz-score" id="quiz-score"></div>
<button class="mark-done" onclick="markDone('s6',this)">○ Mark complete</button>
</div>

</div><!-- .page -->

<script>
// ── section progress ───────────────────────────────────────────
const SEC_LABELS = {
  s1:'Launch paths overview',
  s2:'Dev mode',
  s3:'Docker / browser',
  s4:'Standalone installers',
  s5:'Comparison table',
  s6:'Quiz',
};
const done = {};
const container = document.getElementById('chip-container');
Object.entries(SEC_LABELS).forEach(([id,label])=>{
  const chip = document.createElement('div');
  chip.className='sec-chip';chip.id='chip-'+id;
  chip.textContent=label;
  chip.onclick=()=>{ document.getElementById(id)?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'}); };
  container.appendChild(chip);
});
function markDone(id,btn){
  done[id]=true;
  btn.textContent='✓ Complete';btn.classList.add('done');
  document.getElementById('chip-'+id)?.classList.add('done');
  const n=Object.keys(done).length;
  document.getElementById('progress-pct').textContent=
    `${n} / ${Object.keys(SEC_LABELS).length} sections complete`;
}

// ── quiz ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
function shuffle(a){for(let i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]];}return a;}
let answered=0,correct_count=0;
const total_q=document.querySelectorAll('[data-question]').length;

document.querySelectorAll('[data-question]').forEach(q=>{
  const list=q.querySelector('.options');
  shuffle([...list.children]).forEach(li=>list.appendChild(li));
  const fb=q.querySelector('.feedback');
  list.querySelectorAll('li').forEach(li=>{
    li.tabIndex=0;
    const commit=()=>{
      if(li.classList.contains('locked'))return;
      list.querySelectorAll('li').forEach(x=>x.classList.add('locked'));
      const ok=li.dataset.correct==='true';
      li.classList.add(ok?'correct':'wrong');
      if(!ok)list.querySelector('[data-correct="true"]')?.classList.add('correct');
      fb.style.display='block';fb.className='feedback '+(ok?'correct':'wrong');
      fb.textContent=(ok?'✓ Correct. ':'✗ Not quite. ')+li.dataset.why;
      answered++;if(ok)correct_count++;
      if(answered===total_q){
        const sc=document.getElementById('quiz-score');
        sc.style.display='block';
        sc.textContent=`Score: ${correct_count} / ${total_q}`;
        sc.style.color=correct_count===total_q?'var(--green)':correct_count>=3?'var(--yellow)':'var(--red)';
      }
    };
    li.addEventListener('click',commit);
    li.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(e.key==='Enter'||e.key===' ')commit();});
  });
});
</script>
